## Architecture Considerations

| Component | Implementation |
|-----------|----------------|
| **Trigger** | S3 Event Notification → Lambda → Step Functions |
| **Textract** | AnalyzeDocument with FORMS and TABLES feature types |
| **Comprehend** | Custom classifier for doc type + DetectPIIEntities |
| **Redaction** | Lambda using PDF library to mask coordinates |
| **Storage** | Separate buckets for original and redacted with KMS encryption |
| **Indexing** | DynamoDB with contractId as partition key |
| **Error Handling** | Try-catch per state, DLQ for failed executions |
| **Cost Control** | S3 lifecycle policies, CloudWatch budgets, payload limits |
| **Data Residency** | Regional endpoints, bucket policies enforcing region |
| **IAM** | Role-based access with least privilege scope |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design of an AWS AI pipeline handling privacy, scaling, and cost. Combines managed AI services (Comprehend, SageMaker) with event-driven wiring (S3, Lambda, DynamoDB).

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture across S3, Lambda, and SageMaker
- AI services integration: Comprehend for NER/sentiment, SageMaker for topic modeling
- Data privacy: PII redaction, encryption at rest/in transit, access controls
- Cost awareness: batching, cold starts, endpoint usage, and streaming vs. per-file processing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: demonstrate sequence of calls to AWS services
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend();
const sage = new AWS.SageMakerRuntime();
async function analyze(text){
  const entities = await comprehend.detectEntities({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const sentiment = await comprehend.detectSentiment({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const res = await sage.invokeEndpoint({EndpointName:'topic-model', Body: JSON.stringify({text}), ContentType:'application/json'}).promise();
  return {entities, sentiment, topics: JSON.parse(res.Body.toString())};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures and retries across Lambda, SageMaker, and DynamoDB?
- What security controls and monitoring would you add for regulatory compliance (e.g., encryption, audit logs, IAM least privilege)?

## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests ability to design a privacy-aware, multi-stage pipeline with governance and cross-account considerations.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture; S3 event filtering; Transcribe with diarization; Comprehend PII redaction; SageMaker multi-task inference; DynamoDB composite keys; KMS encryption; Glue lineage; DLQ design; cross-account IAM trust policies.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Orchestrator triggered on S3 PUT event
async function handle(event) {
  const { bucket, key } = event.Records[0].s3;
  const callId = extractCallId(key);
  const transcribeJob = await startTranscribe(bucket, key, callId);
  await pollUntilComplete(transcribeJob.JobId, onTranscribeComplete);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate privacy guarantees end-to-end and simulate PII leakage scenarios?
- How would you detect and mitigate model drift across languages in production?
- What retry and timeout strategies would you implement for each service call?

